1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,470
‘Time waits for no man’ – or so the saying goes.
常言道，“岁月不待人”。

2
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:18,040
We can’t stop time and we can’t control it, although sometimes, in our minds, we think time flies – for example, when the weekend arrives, we feel it’s over in no time!
我们既无法让时间停下脚步，也不能掌控它的流逝，不过有时候在我们的感觉里，时间过得飞快——比如周末明明刚到，转眼就结束了！

3
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:28,960
But if this year in particular feels like it’s passing more quickly than others have done, there could be a scientific explanation why.
但如果你觉得今年的时光流逝得格外快，这背后或许有科学的解释。

4
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:34,990
Of course, we know a year is usually 365 days long.
我们当然都知道，一年通常有365天。

5
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:39,220
Clever scientists calculated this a long time ago.
很久以前，聪慧的科学家们就算出了这个结果。

6
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:48,810
They also worked out that every four years, we need an extra day to keep our calendar in sync – this is called a leap year.
他们还发现，每四年我们需要额外增加一天，才能让日历与地球公转周期保持同步——这一年被称为闰年。

7
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:55,820
But 2021 isn’t one of those years, and yet it’s not behaving like a normal year.
但2021年不是闰年，然而它异于往常。

8
00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:06,290
Scientists and astrophysicists have done the math and discovered the Earth is moving faster than it ever has in the last 50 years.
科学家和天体物理学家经过计算发现，地球的自转速度达到了过去50年来的最快水平。

9
00:01:06,290 --> 00:01:12,650
This means that 2021 is going to be the shortest year in decades.
这意味着2021年将成为几十年来最短的一年。

10
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:23,970
Apparently, this is because the Earth is spinning faster on its axis, quicker than it has done in decades, and the days are therefore shorter.
显然，这是因为地球绕地轴的自转速度创下了数十年以来的新高，所以每一天的时长都变短了。

11
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:34,370
But they are only short by a tiny amount – around 0.05 milliseconds – so don’t panic if you haven’t noticed!
不过这种缩短的幅度极小，大约只有0.05毫秒，所以就算你毫无察觉也不必惊慌！

12
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:39,530
However, long-term these milliseconds add up.
但从长远来看，这些毫秒的差距会不断累积。

13
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:55,300
Astrophysicist Graham Jones and Konstantin Bikos from Time and Date told The Independent newspaper: “If the Earth’s rotation continues to quicken, we may at some point require a negative leap second.
时间与日期网站的天体物理学家格雷厄姆·琼斯和康斯坦丁·比科斯在接受《独立报》采访时表示：“如果地球自转速度持续加快，未来某一天我们或许需要引入负闰秒。

14
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:02,160
If this happens, our clocks would skip a second in order to keep up with the hurrying Earth.”
一旦实施负闰秒，为了跟上地球的自转节奏，我们的时钟就要跳过一秒钟。”

15
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:11,290
Since 1972, 27 leap seconds have only been added to our time and none have been taken away.
自1972年以来，人类只给时间增加过27次闰秒，从未减少过一秒。

16
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:19,420
This really is only of concern for atomic clocks – the most accurate timekeeping devices in history.
这件事其实只关乎原子钟——这种有史以来最精准的计时工具。

17
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:29,840
Studying the Earth’s rotation and then subtracting or adding a leap second to these clocks can ensure they remain aligned and keep us on time.
通过监测地球自转，并相应地给原子钟增减闰秒，就能确保它们与地球自转保持同步，让我们的时间始终准确无误。

18
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:37,420
But now, maybe we need to get a move on before this ‘shorter’ year is gone in a split second!
不过话说回来，或许我们得抓紧时间行动起来，免得这“更短”的一年一眨眼就过去了！
